    <body>
      <sec id="article-28987.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache (SUNHA) is a rare primary headache disorder within the trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias, characterized by very frequent, brief attacks of moderate-to-severe unilateral orbital or temporal pain lasting seconds to minutes. Two phenotypes include short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache with conjunctival injection and tearing (SUNCT) and short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache with cranial autonomic symptoms (SUNA). Proposed mechanisms involve trigeminal&#x02013;autonomic reflex activation and hypothalamic dysfunction, although the exact etiology remains unclear. Secondary causes, such as posterior fossa lesions, pituitary pathology, and vascular compression, must be excluded. Clinical features include high-frequency pain attacks with ipsilateral cranial autonomic symptoms such as lacrimation, conjunctival injection, nasal congestion, or ptosis. Diagnosis is clinical, supported by neuroimaging. Management includes acute and preventive therapies, most commonly lamotrigine, with neurosurgical intervention for refractory cases. Complications include functional impairment and medication-related adverse effects.</p>
        <p>This activity enhances the clinician&#x02019;s ability to evaluate and manage SUNHA by reviewing etiology, risk factors, clinical features, and evidence-based treatment. Participants gain skills in diagnosis, treatment selection, and monitoring while emphasizing exclusion of secondary causes. Training highlights collaboration among neurologists, pain specialists, radiologists, and nursing staff to coordinate imaging, optimize therapy, and support patient education. Interprofessional teamwork improves functional outcomes, reduces complications, and ensures comprehensive care for individuals with SUNHA.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Differentiate between short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache with conjunctival injection and tearing and short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache with cranial autonomic symptoms based on clinical and diagnostic features.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement best practices in managing&#x000a0;short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache to minimize pain episodes and optimize daily functioning.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improve patient understanding of short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache, its triggers, treatment options, and strategies to reduce attack frequency and enhance quality of life.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the interprofessional team to educate, treat, and monitor patients with&#x000a0;short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache to improve&#x000a0;overall health&#x000a0;outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=28987&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=28987">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Headache and pain disorders&#x000a0;are leading causes of morbidity and disability worldwide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r2">[2]</xref> Numerous headache types are categorized by the <italic toggle="yes">International Classification of Headache Disorders, third edition</italic> (ICHD-3), differing in clinical presentation, pathophysiology, treatment strategies, and prognosis. Short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache (SUNHA) is a rare primary headache classified as a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia. Attacks occur unilaterally, affecting orbital, supraorbital, temporal, or other trigeminal-innervated regions and are accompanied by cranial autonomic features.</p>
        <p>Two subtypes of SUNHA are recognized: short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache with conjunctival injection and tearing (SUNCT) and short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache with cranial autonomic symptoms (SUNA).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Although relatively rare, these headache attacks exhibit a characteristic constellation of signs and symptoms, facilitating recognition. Attack duration ranges from 1 to 600 seconds, with a frequency of 1 to 600 episodes per day, averaging fewer than 100 per day.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>SUNCT requires at least 20 episodes, with a minimum of 1 daily attack. Pain is stabbing or pulsating, strictly unilateral, and localized to a trigeminal-innervated area (orbital, supraorbital, or temporal), lasting 1 to 600 seconds, and accompanied by ipsilateral lacrimation and conjunctival injection. SUNA also presents unilaterally but includes only 1 or neither of lacrimation or conjunctival injection, in addition to other cranial autonomic symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>SUNHA belongs to the group of primary headache disorders classified as trigeminal&#x02013;autonomic cephalalgias, with no underlying etiology in typical cases. Symptomatic causes have been identified in some patients, including pituitary adenomas, posterior fossa lesions, and microvascular compressions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The estimated prevalence of SUNCT and SUNA is 6.6 per 100,000, with an annual incidence of 1.2 per 100,000. Age of onset typically ranges from 40 to 70 years, with a mean age of 57. Reviews indicate a male predominance in SUNCT, with a male-to-female ratio of 1.5:1, and a female predominance in SUNA, with a male-to-female ratio of 1:1.7.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r8">[8]</xref> More recent data suggest female predominance in both conditions, with male-to-female ratios of 1:1.2 in SUNCT and 1:2.48 in SUNA. A single case of familial SUNCT has been reported.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The exact pathophysiology of SUNCT and SUNA remains unknown. SUNHA, like other trigeminal&#x02013;autonomic cephalalgias, is thought to involve the trigeminal complex, the brainstem autonomic system, and the hypothalamus. Functional magnetic resonance imaging studies have demonstrated activation of the posterior hypothalamus during SUNCT attacks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>SUNHA is initiated by activation or disinhibition of the trigeminal cervical complex, which stimulates the trigeminal autonomic reflex. The stimulus may be innocuous or noxious, leading to activation of the trigeminal ganglion. The ganglion synapses with the&#x000a0;trigeminal cervical complex, composed of the trigeminal nucleus caudalis and the C1&#x02013;C2 dorsal horns. Neurons then project to the superior salivary nucleus in the pons. Parasympathetic fibers transmit the signal to the sphenopalatine ganglion, which synapses with postganglionic fibers to innervate the lacrimal gland, nasal mucosa, and palate, producing the characteristic constellation of autonomic symptoms associated with SUNHA.</p>
        <p>Posterior hypothalamic activation has been reported in trigeminal&#x02013;autonomic cephalalgias, with recent evidence demonstrating ipsilateral or bilateral activation in patients with SUNCT.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r11">[11]</xref> Hypothalamic dysfunction may result in overproduction of nociceptive orexin B, which activates the trigeminal&#x02013;hypothalamic tract and initiates the pain pathway.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r12">[12]</xref> Secondary activation of cortical areas occurs following trigeminal&#x02013;cervical complex activation, contributing to the severe pain experienced in SUNCT and SUNA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r14">[14]</xref> Deep-brain stimulation of the ventral tegmental area on the side of the attacks produced approximately 80% symptom improvement, supporting the involvement of this structure in SUNCT and SUNA pathophysiology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r15">[15]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The diagnosis of SUNHA is primarily clinical, with investigations used to exclude other causes or secondary forms of the disorder. Diagnostic criteria require at least 20 reported attacks. Headaches are typically described as short-lasting and unilateral, located in the V1 distribution, and&#x000a0;characterized as sharp, stabbing, burning, or electrical shock&#x02013;like pain. Pain intensity ranges from moderate to severe, lasting 1 to 600 seconds, and is localized to the orbital, supraorbital, or temporal regions. Attacks are accompanied by at least 1 cranial autonomic symptom, including conjunctival injection, tearing, rhinorrhea, nasal congestion, eyelid swelling, increased sweating of the head or neck, ear discomfort, miosis, or ptosis.</p>
        <p>SUNCT and SUNA&#x000a0;may be triggered by external stimuli, similar to trigeminal neuralgia. Common precipitating factors include irritation of the trigeminal nerve from chewing, touching the face, tooth brushing, temperature changes, or loud noise. These conditions are distinguished from trigeminal neuralgia by the consistent presence of cranial autonomic symptoms. Migraines and other facial pain syndromes may stimulate parasympathetic ganglia and produce autonomic signs, but these symptoms are generally less pronounced than in SUNCT and SUNA.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Brain MRI with dedicated pituitary views, accompanied by laboratory assessment of pituitary function, constitutes the minimal recommended workup for SUNCT and SUNA&#x000a0;when&#x000a0;structural abnormalities of the pituitary, posterior fossa, or other cranial regions are suspected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Diagnosis remains primarily clinical and is defined by the ICHD-3, using the following criteria:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>A minimum of 20 headache episodes fulfilling the criteria below</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Headache of at least moderate intensity, localized unilaterally to the orbital or temporal region, lasting more than 1 second but less than 600 seconds</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Presence of at least 1 ipsilateral cranial autonomic symptom:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Tearing and conjunctival injection</p></list-item><list-item><p>Nasal congestion or rhinorrhea</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ipsilateral eyelid edema</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ipsilateral sweating of the head and face</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ipsilateral facial or scalp erythema</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ear fullness or discomfort</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ptosis, miosis, or both</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Occurrence of at least 1 attack per day during active disease</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Headache attacks are typically very frequent during active disease. SUNCT and SUNA demonstrate occasional seasonal variation in attack frequency.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Treatments for trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias, including SUNCT and SUNA, fall into 3 categories: bridge&#x000a0;or short-term modalities, long-term preventive approaches, and nonpharmacological interventions. No regimens are currently recommended&#x000a0;for acute attacks due to their extremely brief duration.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Short-Term Therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intravenous lidocaine has demonstrated partial symptom relief in patients with SUNCT and SUNA. Administration requires continuous cardiac and blood pressure monitoring, and it must be performed in an intensive care unit or another monitored inpatient setting. Intravenous lidocaine is contraindicated in patients with cardiac conduction disorders.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Long-Term Prevention</bold>
</p>
        <p>Lamotrigine has demonstrated efficacy as first-line therapy, with up to 80% of patients reporting symptomatic improvement. Effective daily doses range from 100 to 600 mg.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r17">[17]</xref> Carbamazepine and oxcarbazepine are also effective, with doses up to 1200 mg/day. Topiramate, administered at 50 to 500 mg/day, has produced promising results. Gabapentin, at 1800 to 2400 mg/day, has additionally shown clinical benefit in some studies' results.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Nonpharmacological&#x000a0;Procedures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Interventional approaches may be considered&#x000a0;for patients with medically refractory SUNHA. Options include neuromodulation, radiofrequency ablation, &#x003b3; knife radiosurgery, and microvascular decompression. Evidence suggests these strategies may be effective, although data remain limited due to the rarity of the disorder. Greater occipital nerve block has demonstrated symptomatic benefit in some patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r18">[18]</xref> Neuromodulation using noninvasive vagus nerve stimulation has shown efficacy in results from retrospective studies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r19">[19]</xref> Microvascular decompression may be considered in selected medically refractory cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r20">[20]</xref> Lack of response to indomethacin and symptomatic improvement with intravenous lidocaine provide both diagnostic and therapeutic guidance.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>SUNHAs are distinguished by their characteristic constellation of symptoms and brief attack duration. However, exclusion of other headache types with overlapping clinical features is essential. Differential diagnosis should include other trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias, cluster headache, paroxysmal hemicrania, idiopathic chronic facial pain syndrome, atypical migraine, hemicrania continua, trigeminal neuralgia, and retinal migraine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28987.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The natural history of SUNCT remains poorly defined due to its rarity. Onset typically occurs in the fourth or fifth decade of life, with SUNA occasionally progressing to a chronic form over an average of approximately 8 years. The condition is not associated with increased mortality and is not fatal. However, SUNHA can cause significant morbidity and functional disability. Prognosis is improving as additional therapeutic strategies are developed and research advances. Early initiation of treatment is essential to reduce attack frequency and severity, thereby optimizing clinical outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>SUNCT does not produce long-term neurological sequelae or complications. Most patients remain asymptomatic between attacks. Although the social and economic burden of SUNCT has not been fully quantified, severe cases have been associated with functional disability.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>No specific risk factors have been identified, and no established prevention exists for SUNHA and related syndromes. In some patients, background interictal pain may result from underlying migrainous biology or analgesic overuse syndrome. Patient education regarding the risks of analgesic overuse is essential to prevent additional headache attacks. Excessive use of analgesic medications can exacerbate the condition by increasing both attack frequency and severity.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28987.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Accurate diagnosis of SUNCT and SUNA is essential because many conditions present with similar clinical features, yet management strategies differ markedly in terms of action mechanism and effectiveness. Comprehensive history taking is essential for distinguishing SUNHAs from other headache types. Thorough evaluation to exclude secondary causes of facial pain, including vascular compression of the trigeminal nerve, is necessary and may alter overall management.</p>
        <p>All members of the healthcare team must be familiar with this condition&#x000a0;to ensure&#x000a0;appropriate management and prevent disability. Treatment of SUNCT requires an interprofessional approach involving the primary clinician, neurologist, nursing staff, and pharmacy. Early referral to a neurologist facilitates timely diagnosis and initiation of management due to the debilitating nature of the condition.</p>
      </sec>
